#!/usr/bin/perl 

use strict;
use Pod::Usage;
pod2usage(-exitstatus => 0, -verbose => 2);

__END__

=head1 NAME

qesub - submit job(s) relating to elctronic structure theory (EST) program to SLURM. 

=head1 SYNOPSIS

I<qesub> [ options ] I<infile> 

=head2 Infile:

Four modes are provided:

=over 5

=item 1. I<$infile> = B<filename>; submit a single job

 ]$ qesub molecule.com

=item 2. I<$infile> = 'B<all>'; submit all jobs in the current folder

 ]$ qesub all

=item 3. I<$infile> = 'B<fail>'; resubmit only the jobs that failed last time

 ]$ qesub fail

=item 4. I<$infile> = B<*.dat>; submit the jobs listed in a I<.dat> file (one
job name per line)

 ]$ qesub joblist.dat

=back

=head2 Options:

=head3 General:

=over 8

=item -h, --help

Show this help message and exit.

=back

=head3 EST related:

=over 8

=item -e B<orca|g16|jellyfish|qchem|molpro>

Assign the EST program (default: guessed from the input file extension).

=back

=head3 SLURM related:

=over 8

=item -np B<procs>

Invoke a parallel job with B<procs> cpus.

=item -p B<partition>[,B<partition2>,...]

Specify the SLURM partition. With B<-w all>, this may instead be a
comma-separated list (e.g. C<mpp.share,mpp.single,smp>) to sweep every node
across all of those partitions in a single invocation -- each node is still
submitted into its own correct partition.

=item -t B<time>

Specify the time limit for the job. Passed straight through to SLURM's
C<--time>, which accepts several formats (hours are not capped at 24 in the
plain forms, so C<48:00:00> is just as valid as C<2-00:00:00>):

 MM               minutes
 MM:SS            minutes:seconds
 HH:MM:SS         hours:minutes:seconds   (default: 24:00:00)
 D-HH             days-hours
 D-HH:MM          days-hours:minutes
 D-HH:MM:SS       days-hours:minutes:seconds

Use the C<D-HH...> form when a limit is easier to read in days, e.g. a
C<-w>/C<-N> run over a large job list that needs more than a day per node.

=item -m B<mem>

Specify the memory allocation for the job (e.g. 8GB).

=item -w B<node>|B<all>|B<node1,node2,...>

Sweep mode: submit the I<entire> job list once per node, each pinned via
C<--nodelist>, instead of splitting the job list across nodes. C<all> expands
to every node currently in B<-p> (which may itself list several partitions).
Each node's output goes into its own C<sweep_NODE/> directory so runs don't
collide.

Use this to test a program/binary across every node of a partition, e.g.
after a module update -- follow up with C<checkEst -r refDir sweep> to check
the results.

=item -N B<n>|B<auto>

Distribute (not replicate) the job list across B<n> batches, non-interactively
-- skips the "how many batch files?" prompt. C<auto> sets B<n> to the number
of nodes currently in B<-p>, capped at the job count.

Batches are I<not> pinned to a node (no C<--nodelist>): SLURM's own scheduler
spreads them across whichever nodes are free -- one job per node if there are
enough free nodes, otherwise several jobs per node. Mutually exclusive with
B<-w>.

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

 Submit an ORCA job with 1 core (Minimum Syntax):
 ]$ qesub molecule.inp 

 Submit an ORCA job with 8 cores:
 ]$ qesub -np 8 molecule.inp 

 Submit all the jobs in the current directory:
 ]$ qesub all

 Sweep all jobs across every node of mpp.share (e.g. verifying a Gaussian
 module update on every node, one full test-suite run per node):
 ]$ qesub -e g16 -p mpp.share -w all all

 Sweep all jobs across every node of every AMD partition in one call
 (no loop needed, so nothing gets submitted twice):
 ]$ qesub -e g16 -p mpp.share,mpp.single,smp -w all all

 Distribute (not replicate) all jobs across mpp.share automatically, one
 batch per node in the partition, unpinned (SLURM schedules each batch):
 ]$ qesub -e g16 -p mpp.share -N auto all

 Sweep a large test suite (each node runs the full list sequentially) with
 a 2-day time limit instead of the 24h default:
 ]$ qesub -e g16 -p mpp.share -w all -t 2-00:00:00 all

